"You'll own nothing and be happy" - [World Economic Forum](https://www.weforum.org)
You'll own nothing and be happy" is a phrase published by the World Economic Forum (WEF) and based on a 2016 essay by the Danish politician Ida Auken about a future in which a hypothetical person relies on the sharing economy for many of their needs. (Wikipedia)

This phrase gets more accurate, day by day.

# The End Of Ownership

Imagine this: It's 2002 and your favorite singer has just released their new song. You pop into a store and get their new CD, with their whole album on it.
**You own this CD.**
You can always play this CD, as long as you have a CD player. It doesn't matter if you're in Tokyo, or in the Antarctic, you can play the song.
These days are over. Today, we are mostly buying licenses.

## You don't own the game, you own the temporary permission to play.

Have you ever bought a game digitally? If yes, then congratulations, you fell victim to the new age of "renting".
Every time, you buy a game digitally, no matter if you do it on the PlayStation Store, Steam or even the PlayStore, you're not buying the game, but a license instead.
Sony, Microsoft, Epic Games, Steam and all other companies that offer their services to you, selling games, have the legal right to take away your license at any time. They can take away your game, that you bought and paid for, at any time.
Think about that for a second.
Most famous example of this is the first itteration of "The Crew" series. In March of 2024, Ubisoft made the decision to permanently shut down servers, as well as taking away the game from peoples library, technically killing their license.

# A CEO's wet dream

Why do you think everything is subscription based?
It's predictable, that's why.
Companies love predictable revenue. Especially public companies and their shareholders/investors.
A purchase is a one time transaction, therefore unpredictable, whereas a subscription gives the companies safety, with them knowing how much money they'll roughly get, and when they will get it.
The companies piss on the consumer for their own personal gain. Nothing new, is it?
Wall Street rewards recurring revenue models with higher valuations, so companies are financially incentivized to lock you in.

# Welcome back, Cable TV!

Netflix was supposed to be the big antidote to paying for cable TV channels you didn't watch.
Now Cable TV is back, under a new umbrella. Today we have a ton of streaming services:
- Netflix
- Amazon Prime
- Disney Plus
- HBO Max
- Apple TV+
- Peacock
- Hulu
- Joyn
- Paramount+

and the list goes on... Do you see the issue?
Some Movies are only available on Peacock, but others only exist on HBO. Season 1 of this show is exclusive to Netflix, the others are on Prime...
This is the new cable TV, but more fragmented than any cable TV could be.
53% of US Households have four or more streaming subscriptions active, thats up to $70 a month!! [Source: mountain.com](https://mountain.com/blog/best-streaming-services/) 
